WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.050
Moving a service account or its S P N to
Entra changes which certificates exist.

2
00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:08.113
Each row starts from what
the workload holds today,

3
00:00:08.113 --> 00:00:12.487
and an S P N needs no certificate, as
the service accounts guide explains.

4
00:00:12.487 --> 00:00:14.188
Start with SQL Server.

5
00:00:14.188 --> 00:00:18.866
An engine running as a user account
or a G M S A, with Windows logins,

6
00:00:18.866 --> 00:00:22.750
has an optional T L S
certificate; Kerberos needs none.

7
00:00:22.750 --> 00:00:27.194
Add Entra logins, and every instance that
takes them needs a T L S certificate

8
00:00:27.194 --> 00:00:31.850
issued by a certificate authority, with
the service account able to read its key.

9
00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:34.871
CyberArk Certificate Manager
issues and renews it,

10
00:00:34.871 --> 00:00:38.313
from the program's Active
Directory Certificate Services.

11
00:00:38.313 --> 00:00:43.623
A SQL Server 2022 instance's Entra identity
adds an app-registration certificate in

12
00:00:43.623 --> 00:00:48.425
Key Vault, which Key Vault and the Arc
extension handle when it's service-managed.

13
00:00:48.425 --> 00:00:52.688
A SQL Server 2025 instance
uses the Arc managed identity:

14
00:00:52.688 --> 00:00:56.525
no certificate before, none
after, and Azure handles it.

15
00:00:56.525 --> 00:01:00.082
An I I S pool published through
application proxy or Private

16
00:01:00.082 --> 00:01:04.137
Access with Kerberos single sign-on
keeps the same site certificate.

17
00:01:04.137 --> 00:01:08.625
The connector reaches the S P N with
Kerberos, so there's no new certificate.

18
00:01:08.625 --> 00:01:12.293
CyberArk Certificate Manager
handles the site's certificate,

19
00:01:12.293 --> 00:01:15.550
and Microsoft handles the
application proxy endpoint.
